1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,526
Muse, Grok Bot, and now dots.

2
00:00:03,526 --> 00:00:11,035
They all sell the same shape of thing: an agent that works while you're away, brings you finished work, and learns from your feedback.

3
00:00:12,035 --> 00:00:21,080
I've been running a small version of that loop on my laptop since before any of them launched. It's not a competitor. It's just a loop I find useful.

4
00:00:22,080 --> 00:00:23,339
I call it elves.

5
00:00:24,339 --> 00:00:31,443
You know the story. The shoemaker goes to bed. In the morning, the shoes are finished. All he does is look them over.

6
00:00:32,443 --> 00:00:34,747
That's the whole design. Here's the loop.

7
00:00:35,747 --> 00:00:42,936
Every hour, a scheduled job scans the inboxes and calendars you named in one config file. Nothing else.

8
00:00:43,936 --> 00:00:49,333
Anything that needs real work gets drafted right away, without asking. Nothing is sent.

9
00:00:50,333 --> 00:00:55,880
After lunch, you open review. Every item starts with the same line: action taken.

10
00:00:56,880 --> 00:01:01,147
You approve, edit, redraft, or discard. That's your entire job.

11
00:01:02,147 --> 00:01:10,979
Your reactions become rules. "Too long, Hamin just wants the number" becomes one line on Hamin's page, and the next draft is shorter.

12
00:01:11,979 --> 00:01:14,070
Five reasons I run it this way.

13
00:01:15,070 --> 00:01:20,382
Scheduled jobs, not a cloud computer. It's always on while the laptop is awake.

14
00:01:21,382 --> 00:01:30,150
A database you own. Every task and every piece of feedback is a row in a sqlite file you can query, back up, or delete.

15
00:01:31,150 --> 00:01:36,697
Full control. It reads only what's in one config file, and nothing sends without you.

16
00:01:37,697 --> 00:01:51,798
A second brain you can read. Everything it learns lands in a markdown wiki, a page per person, project, and output type, and every rule cites the feedback it came from. Open it in Obsidian and you can see all of it.

17
00:01:52,798 --> 00:02:02,675
And any coding agent, including the one you already have at work. You may not be able to use Muse, Grok Bot, or dots on a work machine, and you don't need to.

18
00:02:03,675 --> 00:02:14,555
The whole design is one markdown file, about 200 lines. Hand it to Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, or whatever your company already allows, and it builds it.

19
00:02:15,555 --> 00:02:17,902
It's on GitHub. Link in the post.
